      "report_date": "2026-11-16",
      "title": "Hackers Disguise LOTUSLITE V2 Malware as Indo-Pacific Security Assessment to Compromise Windows Systems",
      "summary": "A Thailand-focused campaign distributes LOTUSLITE V2, a .NET successor to the C++ LOTUSLITE backdoor, disguised as an Indo-Pacific economic and security assessment. The intrusion starts with a public Zoho WorkDrive link to an archive, and executing its launcher stages a signed PDFgear executable that side-loads a malicious DLL. The malware establishes persistence through a Run key when running as SYSTEM or a user Load value otherwise, and it hijacks the PdfGear document file-association command. Its encrypted command-and-control uses AES-256-GCM over WebSockets and a new PRO1 protocol marker, so older signatures may miss it. The Mustang Panda link is an analytical assessment, not confirmed attribution. Defenders should correlate DeviceSync staging, suspicious PDFgear child processes, and persistence changes rather than relying only on network signatures.",
      "completeness": "Low",
      "threats": [
        "Lotuslite",
        "Dll_sideloading_technique"
      ],
