  {
    "parsed": {
      "report_date": "2026-11-16",
      "title": "WPPConnect Compromised: Malicious Releases Steal Secrets and WhatsApp Sessions Through the Project's Own Pipeline",
      "summary": "Malicious releases of two WPPConnect npm packages, @wppconnect-team/wppconnect 2.4.1 and @wppconnect/server 2.10.82, shipped a diagnostics module that steals environment variables, .env and config files, and WhatsApp session tokens. The payload runs on import or server start, so npm install --ignore-scripts does not prevent it. The malicious code was built and published through the project's own GitHub Actions pipeline with valid provenance, after unreviewed commits were pushed to default branches. The project then force-pushed history and deleted tags, so tagged source on GitHub no longer matched the published tarballs. Affected Docker images, nightly release assets, and CI jobs that imported the library are also exposed. Defenders should search lockfiles and image digests, treat any exposed secrets and WhatsApp sessions as compromised, pin to library 2.4.0 and server 2.10.81, and add a release-age delay and egress allowlist.",
      "completeness": "Low",
      "threats": [
