      "title": "CyberXtron Researchers Uncover Fake Parivahan Campaign Delivering Grok-Assisted Android Banking Trojan",
      "summary": "A fake e-Challan traffic-fine campaign targeting Indian vehicle owners uses at least 64 lookalike domains to deliver a multi-stage Android banking trojan. The dropper shows a fake Google Play install screen and decrypts further stages in memory before installing a full remote-access trojan. Once Accessibility access is granted, the operator can stream the screen over hidden VNC, log keystrokes, overlay payment apps such as PhonePe, and intercept SMS and OTPs. The malware can also steal and reset lock-screen credentials on many phone brands, and it sends stolen SMS and app lists to the xAI Grok API for automated triage. The report links the command-and-control design to the Groklem and ArcticRAT family but does not attribute the campaign to a named actor. Defenders should watch for the multi-port C2 pattern, Accessibility and SMS permission combinations, and outbound calls to AI and messaging APIs from sideloaded apps.",
